UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 1: Creating a movement sequence', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end. Ideas can come from a theme such as weather, animals or machines. Choose movements, link them smoothly and use levels (high, middle, low), speed (fast, slow) and direction (forwards, backwards, sideways). Practise so the sequence can be repeated. Use a clear space and never push or pull others.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose a theme (such as ''Robot'') and write a beginning, middle and end that uses one level change and one speed change.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides. Middle: stiff, fast arm movements at a high level, then slow marching at a low level. End: power down by slowly crouching. The sequence includes level and speed changes.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a sequence needs lots of complicated movements. A few clear, well-linked movements are better.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a sequence of 6 to 8 movements on your own theme with a clear beginning, middle and end. Write the movements, levels, speeds and directions in a chart. Practise five times and perform it for family, then ask for two stars and a wish.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end. Ideas can come from a theme such as weather, animals or machines. Choose movements, link them smoothly and use levels (high, middle, low), speed (fast, slow) and direction (forwards, backwards, sideways). Practise so the sequence can be repeated. Use a clear space and never push or pull others.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a sequence of 6 to 8 movements on your own theme with a clear beginning, middle and end. Write the movements, levels, speeds and directions in a chart. Practise five times and perform it for family, then ask for t Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements.. Correction: Sample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements. A few clear, well-linked movements are better. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides"],"question":"Choose a theme (such as ''Robot'') and write a beginning, middle and end that uses one level change and one speed change. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a theme; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides","Careful use of beginning, middle and end and explain wh with clear evidence","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements","A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named beginning, middle and end and explain wh, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name beginning, middle and end and explain wh only, without linking it to a theme","Apply a theme to new material and name the beginning, middle and end and explain wh used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements"],"question":"Which response best applies a theme with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a theme","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example theme ''Thunderstorm''","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Create a sequence of 6 to 8 movements on your own theme with a clear beginning, middle and end","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements","Name beginning, middle and end and explain wh only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a theme to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements.. Correction: Sample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w34-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a theme.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use a theme with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify beginning, middle and end and explain why it matters.","I can practise levels and speed independently on a new prompt.","I can notice linking movements needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end.","options":["A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like rain, fast steps for wind, then a big jump for thunder with a soft landing. End: slowly drift to the floor and be still. The dancer uses levels (high, low) and speeds (fast, slow).\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose a theme (such as ''Robot'') and write a beginning, middle and end that uses one level change and one speed change.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides. Middle: stiff, fast arm movements at a high level, then slow marching at a low level. End: power down by slowly crouching. The sequence includes level and speed changes.","explain":"A movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end. Ideas can come from a theme such as weather, animals or machines. Choose movements, link them smoothly and use levels (high, middle, low), speed (fast, slow) and direction (forwards, backwards, sideways). Practise so the sequence can be repeated. Use a clear space and never push or pull others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements. A few clear, well-linked movements are better. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A themed sequence.","caption":"Three clear parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Theme</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Thunderstorm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Parts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">begin, middle, end</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like rain, fast steps for wind, then a big jump for thunder with a soft landing. End: slowly drift to the floor and be still. The dancer uses levels (high, low) and speeds (fast, slow). Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Levels and speeds.","caption":"Vary level and speed.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">high, middle, low</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Speed</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fast, slow</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a sequence needs lots of complicated movements. A few clear, well-linked movements are better. Here is a more helpful example: Example theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like rain, fast steps for wind, then a big jump for thunder with a soft landing. End: slowly drift to the floor and be still. The dancer uses levels (high, low) and speeds (fast, slow).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Beginning: stand still with arms by your sides. Middle: stiff, fast arm movements at a high level, then slow marching at a low level. End: power down by slowly crouching. The sequence includes level and speed changes.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a sequence needs lots of complicated movements. A few clear, well-linked movements are better. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA movement sequence has a clear beginning, middle and end. Ideas can come from a theme such as weather, animals or machines. Choose movements, link them smoothly and use levels (high, middle, low), speed (fast, slow) and direction (forwards, backwards, sideways). Practise so the sequence can be repeated. Use a clear space and never push or pull others. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 1: Creating a movement sequence","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Gymnastics and dance","week":34,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample theme ''Thunderstorm''. Beginning: stand still and curl up small (the calm before). Middle: shake hands high like rain, fast steps for wind, then a big jump for thunder with a soft landing. End: slowly drift to the floor and be still. The dancer uses levels (high, low) and speeds (fast, slow).\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A themed sequence.","caption":"Three clear parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Theme</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Thunderstorm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Parts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">begin, middle, end</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Levels and speeds.","caption":"Vary level and speed.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">high, middle, low</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Speed</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fast, slow</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w34-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
